 'order of Main.java: the three enums, the Money helpers, the records (User, Participant, Share, Transfer), the Clock, '
 'the SplitStrategy interface with its three rules and the CheckedSplit wrapper, the SimplifyStrategy with min cash '
 'flow, the observer, Expense, Ledger, then Group with its lock and the order at addExpense, then the thin Splitwise '
 'caller, then a main with fifty threads.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Product wants a fourth way to split: by shares, 2:1:1, so a couple pays double a single.", "functional", 10,
 "The split rule is already an interface, so this is one new class and one line. It adds up the weights, gives each "
 "participant total &times; weight / units rounded down, and hands the leftover paise to the last participant so the "
 "shares still come out to the bill. Nothing else moves: the group, the ledger, the lock and the tests are untouched, "
 "and CheckedSplit polices the new rule exactly as it polices the old ones. The one seam that is not free is that "
 "SplitType is an enum, so a brand-new constant is the single extra edit; key the registry by String and even that "
 "disappears.",
 X("a fourth way to split", "fairness")),
("Two people add an expense to the same group at the same instant. Prove you cannot lose one, with a test.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between reading a balance and writing it back. addExpense does the whole read-modify-write &mdash; "
 "the id check, the membership check and every net and pair update &mdash; inside one lock held by the group, so no "
 "other writer can run in that gap. The proof is arithmetic rather than a count: fifty threads wait on one latch, all "
 "fifty post an expense to the same five-person group, and the test then adds up every balance in the ledger. If a "
 "single update had been lost the total would be non-zero, because a lost credit has no matching lost debit. It also "
 "cross-checks the two maps against each other for every member.",
 T("        // 1. fifty people post an expense", "        // 2. an exact split")),
("One lock per group. Does that scale, or have you serialised Splitwise?", "non-functional", 5,
 "It scales, and the answer is arithmetic. The locked part is about twenty hash operations &mdash; an id lookup, a "
 "membership check, one net update and one pair update per participant &mdash; about two microseconds. A busy trip "
 "group posts forty expenses a day; even a fictional group posting a hundred a second holds the lock two hundred "
 "microseconds in every second, two hundredths of one per cent. And the lock is per group, so the number of groups is "
 "irrelevant: ten million of them never meet. The ladder, in order: notification and persistence already run outside "
 "the lock; a group with thousands of members gets one atomic merge per pair on a concurrent map keyed by the two ids "
 "in sorted order; and beyond one process each pair becomes a database row updated with SET paise = paise + ?, which "
 "is the database performing the same atomic step.",
 X("persistence", "the simplification is a suggestion")),
("The exact amounts they typed do not add up to the bill. What is the state of the system?", "functional", 10,
 "Exactly what it was. The shares are computed and checked before the lock is even taken, so a bill of five hundred "
 "with shares adding up to four hundred throws from the split rule and the group never reaches its ledger. Inside the "
 "lock the remaining checks &mdash; has this expense id been used, is every participant a member &mdash; also happen "
 "before any write, and what follows them is map arithmetic that cannot fail half way. So there is no partial "
 "expense, no half-updated pair and nothing to roll back: the client fixes the numbers and retries. The wrapper is the "
 "part worth pointing at: it polices rules that do not exist yet, which the failure tests prove by registering a "
 "deliberately broken rule and showing that nothing was written.",
 sect(src, "class CheckedSplit", "// \"settle with the fewest")),
("Three people split a bill of one rupee. Where does the last paise go, and how do you know it is not lost?", "functional", 5,
 "A hundred paise over three is 33 each with one left over, so the shares are 34, 33 and 33 and the extra paise goes "
 "to the payer &mdash; the person who fronted the money, chosen because it is a rule you can say out loud and repeat. "
 "The order is deterministic (payer first, then everyone else in the order the client sent), so the same expense "
 "always splits the same way, which matters the moment you replay a log. The proof that nothing is lost is not the "
 "splitting code: it is the wrapper, which adds the shares up and refuses anything that is not exactly the bill, plus "
 "a failure test that checks the shares sum to 100 and the balances sum to zero. A fairness variant that rotates who "
 "absorbs the odd paise is in Extensions.java.",
 sect(src, "final class Remainder", "class ExactSplit") + "\n" + X("fairness", "multi-currency")),
("Somebody edited last week's expense. Then deleted another one. What happens to the balances?", "twist", 10,
 "Both are reversals, and both are exact, because each expense stores the shares it was applied with instead of "
 "recomputing them. A delete applies those stored shares with the sign flipped and marks the row DELETED; the row "
 "stays in the log, so the audit trail is intact and a second delete is refused rather than double-reversed. An edit "
 "is the same reversal followed immediately by the new version, both inside one lock, so nobody can read a group where "
 "the old expense is gone and the new one has not arrived; the old row is marked EDITED and version n+1 is appended. "
 "As with adding, the new shares are computed before the lock, so an edit that does not add up changes nothing at all.",
 sect(src, "Expense edit(", "long netOf(String userId)")),
("Settle up, and then show me the fewest payments that would clear the whole group.", "twist", 10,
 "A settle-up is an expense of a special kind: Bob paying Alice is a bill paid by Bob with exactly one participant, "
 "Alice, owing all of it, so it takes the same path, the same lock and the same reversal, and a partial payment needs "
 "no special case at all &mdash; pay twenty of the fifty you owe and thirty is left. Simplify is different in kind: it "
 "takes a snapshot of the nets and runs greedy min cash flow over two heaps, repeatedly making the biggest debtor pay "
 "the biggest creditor as much as clears one of them, which needs at most n-1 payments. It returns a suggestion and "
 "touches nothing, so the pairwise history stays auditable; the failure test checks that every net is identical "
 "afterwards and that paying the plan really does clear everybody. The honest caveat: greedy is not provably minimal "
 "&mdash; the exact minimum is a subset-sum problem &mdash; which is why the product calls it simplified, not minimal.",
 sect(src, "class MinCashFlow", "// \"the others must be told\"") + "\n" + X("the simplification is a suggestion", "auto-simplify")),
("The app asks \"what do I owe Bob?\" a thousand times a second. Make it O(1).", "non-functional", 5,
 "No new structure. The ledger already keeps a net per person and a pairwise map, updated as each expense lands, so "
 "both questions are one hash lookup; nothing ever re-reads the expense log to compute a balance. The two maps are "
 "written by one method from one signed number, which is why A-owes-B and B-owes-A cannot disagree, and a pair that "
